      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-94695",
      ":question/problem": "The heights (in meters) of the trees in a particular park are normally distributed with a mean of $9\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{m}.$ Assuming that the heights of the trees in the park are normally distributed, what is the probability that a randomly selected tree is less than $10\\,\\text{m}$ tall? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\u03a6(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\u03a6(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |"
