      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-324314",
      ":question/problem": "The lengths of metal rods produced by a certain factory are normally distributed with a mean of $100$ centimeters and a standard deviation of $4$ centimeters. What is the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\u03a6(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\u03a6(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}."
